{
  "id": "q-86587",
  "parts": [
    {
      "part": "problem",
      "original_record": "before",
      "value": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071504/assets/02dabe7099c201f717885a05842e0cb8543bd01cccf0d6c52147f674cde15dce.png)\n\nLet $U=\\{a,b,c,20,40\\}$ be the universal set and let $R=\\{b,20,40\\}.$ What is the complement of $R?$",
      "original_html_sha256": "b2c19affb3d9c04b35a9123ce225a871764afc5e5d484e707574e553cda2514b",
      "extractor_sha256": "1591183da328a4140792793d2cc113fd535ab5f25bc7f284ceb1b6f251efad18",
      "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071504/repairs/source-provenance-v2/structured-source/source/b2c19affb3d9c04b35a9123ce225a871764afc5e5d484e707574e553cda2514b.html",
      "extraction_errors": []
    },
    {
      "part": "worked_solution",
      "original_record": "history",
      "value": "The complement of $R,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{R},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $R.$ To represent $\\overset{―}{R},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $R,$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071504/assets/79900f22c35570b35cf10664768ecf4460a7953ddf7f91dac1893b3ce81339bf.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $a$ and $c.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $R.$ Therefore, the complement of $R$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{R}=\\{a,c\\}.\n$$",
      "original_html_sha256": "2278bc77877836aeedb39579dc1a971dec5ad3d1a5926f86addf78a09546e347",
      "extractor_sha256": "1591183da328a4140792793d2cc113fd535ab5f25bc7f284ceb1b6f251efad18",
      "source_file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071504/repairs/source-provenance-v2/structured-source/source/2278bc77877836aeedb39579dc1a971dec5ad3d1a5926f86addf78a09546e347.html",
      "extraction_errors": []
    }
  ]
}
